# Security toolkit (desktop/security)

- `catalog.toml` is the single source of truth: each tool has a tier (top10/default; large and everything are Kali metapackages in the container), a Kali menu category and one source.
- Source order of preference: `apt` (Ubuntu 26.04 package) → `pipx` (isolated Python env, PyPI name or `git+https` URL) → `github` (static release binary; refused unless the release publishes a checksums file and the SHA-256 matches) → `kali` (Kali rolling inside the `ambar-kali` distrobox).
- Check new apt names exist in Ubuntu 26.04 before adding them. `tests/test_ambar_sec.py` fails if any package in `kali-default.txt` is neither covered nor listed under `[excluded]` with a reason.
- Packages that start a network service must be added to `[services] disable`. The boot test fails the Security ISO if apache2, smbd or snmpd are enabled.
- Never weaken the checksum check, never run the Kali container as root, and never expose these tools to Ambar automations or the AI (their action set stays closed).
- The `ambar-security` app only builds an `ambar-sec` command and runs it in Konsole; keep privileged work in `ambar-sec`.
- Docs: `docs/security.md`. Keep the authorised-use notice.
